%% ABSTRACT-BEGIN
\begin{abstract}
On-scalp optically pumped magnetometers (OPMs) record larger fields than cryogenic magnetoencephalography (MEG), but in a spherical head their signal-to-noise ratio is higher only for sources shallower than a depth set by the sensor-noise ratio. Here, with explicit sensor, brain and room noise, we compared the << F.g2_squid_channels >>-channel Neuromag system with a dense and a site-matched OPM array (at Neuromag's sites) by matched-filter detectability in an adult and << F.wr_n_smaller_heads_words >> smaller heads (<< F.wr_n_scaled_words >> scaled adults, << F.wr_n_templates_words >> infant templates, << F.wr_n_children_words >> children). In the adult, with sensor plus brain noise, the dense << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>-fT/√Hz array had << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >> times Neuromag's detectability (interval: spatial variation), an advantage not established at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >>~fT/√Hz; the site-matched array showed no established advantage. Measured noise raised Neuromag's detectability << F.rev_cov_det_combined_corrected_ratio >>-fold over the model, so the dense advantage awaits measured OPM noise. Under the modeled noise (exploratory), the dense advantage was smallest in cingulate cortex, and the site-matched array trailed on ventral and polar surfaces. In the fixed adult helmet, the scaled adults and templates gained << F.g3b_templates_scaled_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB over the adult, and << F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_fittedtop_vs_top_range >>~dB with each head's helmet fitted at the adult's top-contact gap; the children, with unverified near-scalp anatomy, are provisional. In a confirmatory simulation (idealized detector, modeled noise, fixed adult helmet), the dense array detected spikes << F.cf_band >>~mm deep at << F.cf_all_dense_vs_combined_practical_reduction_pct_range >> lower strength than Neuromag in all << F.wr_n_anatomies_words >> anatomies (not independent replications); exploratory localization established no difference. Clinical reports of higher OPM signal-to-noise ratios are compatible with, but not explained by, these conditions.
\end{abstract}
