\subsection{Sensor arrays and helmets}\label{sec:arrays}

The Neuromag system (<< F.g2_squid_channels >> channels) combined the sample recording's VectorView sensor geometry with the T3 coil definitions of MNE-Python (types << F.meth_coil_grad_type >> and << F.meth_coil_mag_type >>). Its white noise was the typical value of the TRIUX datasheet, << F.meth_squid_mag_asd >>~fT/√Hz for the magnetometers and << F.meth_squid_grad_asd >>~fT/(cm~√Hz) for the gradiometers, with an << F.meth_dewar_mm >>-mm Dewar spacing between coils and helmet surface \citep{elekta_triux}. No signal-space separation was applied.

Each OPM was a single-axis sensor whose sensing center lay nominally << F.g2_dense_standoff_mm >>~mm above the MRI scalp \citep{alem2023,osborne2018}, with a few sites moved outward for clearance. Its field was averaged over a << F.g2_opm_cell_mm >>-mm cubic volume, a modeling choice that changes the peak field by at most << F.wr_opm_cell_peak_effect_pct >>. Its sensitive axis was the head-surface normal averaged within << F.meth_opm_axis_radius_mm >>~mm of the site; with nearly radial axes, the dense array's ratio with the room field projected out (Section~\ref{sec:noise}) would be << F.dp("meth_hse_radial_dense_proj_ratio_3dp", 2) >> instead of << F.dp("meth_hse_final_dense_proj_ratio_3dp", 2) >> (Section~S3).

OPMs covered the scalp above the plane through the preauricular points and a point << F.wr_cover_brow_offset_mm >>~mm above the nasion, keeping at least << F.g2_preauricular_exclusion_mm >>~mm from either preauricular point. The dense OPM array filled this region at a minimum spacing of << F.g2_dense_packing_mm >>~mm (<< F.g2_dense_sites >> sites on the adult, more sites than any clinical OPM array). The site-matched OPM array placed one OPM at each Neuromag site whose projection onto the scalp fell inside it (<< F.g2_matched_sites >> of << F.g2_squid_sites >> sites; Fig.~\ref{fig:arrays}A--C). On every smaller head, the dense array was rebuilt by the same rules (<< F.g3b_smaller_dense_sites_range >> sites), and the site-matched array took that head's Neuromag sites at top contact (<< F.g3b_smaller_matched_sites_range >> sites; Table~\ref{tab:anatomies}).

Clinical systems reach about 100--128 channels with dual- or triaxial sensors at fewer sites \citep{feys2025,shen2026}. We did not model such arrays; a triaxial array at the site-matched sites (<< F.g2_triax_channels >> channels) served as a control for the gain from extra axes. The OPM white noise was << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz in the primary analysis, flat over << F.g2_band_range_hz >>~Hz, and was varied over << F.wr_opm_asd_sweep_list >>~fT/√Hz (the clinical studies state their noise figures without a band).

The fixed adult helmet kept the adult's sensors and noise for every head (Fig.~\ref{fig:arrays}D). In its primary placement, top contact, the head was taken from the pose that puts its head frame where the adult's was recorded and raised until the nearest magnetometer coil was << F.g3b_cfg_clearance >>~mm from the scalp, a best-fit assumption that favors Neuromag \citep{gaetz2008,tesan2010}. A family of << F.g3b_n_family_placements >> source-blind placements, chosen from the geometry alone (contacts, shifts and rotations; Section~S5), gave a placement band.

To separate head size from helmet fit, we also placed each head in a helmet fitted at the adult's gap. The head was moved sideways onto the helmet's midline (laterally centered), and the helmet was scaled about the head origin until the median magnetometer-to-scalp gap equaled the adult's in the same pose, << F.rev_cgap_adult_gap_mm >>~mm. For comparison, the adult's gap is << F.g2_squid_scalp_dist_median_mm >>~mm at its measured head position and << F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_gap_mm >>~mm at top contact. No coil could come within the << F.g3b_cfg_dewar_spacing >>-mm Dewar spacing of the scalp; in child C, this limit bound first, at << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_gap_mm >>~mm. None of these constructions models a pediatric SQUID system.

We compared the two helmets by the within-head contrast, the median over a head's targets of the target-wise difference in $D$ between the fixed and the fitted helmet, and by the interaction, a smaller head's contrast minus the adult's. Both compare two Neuromag helmets around the same OPM array, so the OPM terms cancel target by target and neither depends on the assumed OPM noise. Three secondary controls were a variant fitted at the adult's top-contact gap (<< F.rev_cgap_adult_top_gap_mm >>~mm), a helmet scaled with the head, which scales the gap too, and an equal OPM site count (Section~S5). For the last, the adult's dense array was subsampled to each smaller head's number of sites.

