\subsection{The noise model against measured Neuromag noise}\label{sec:res-noise}

The model predicted << F.rev_cov_brain_mag_amp_ratio >> of the magnetometers' measured brain-noise amplitude, an independent prediction (Fig.~S2), or << F.rev_cov_heart_mag_amp_without_ratio >> with the heart's field removed. The gradiometer level at randomly held-out sites (<< F.rev_cov_heldout_random_grad_amp_ratio >>; << F.rev_cov_heldout_random_grad_amp_ci >> over << F.rev_cov_heldout_n_splits >> splits) and the overall pattern of channel correlations agreed with the measurement, the former nearly by construction. The modeled brain noise was, however, too flat from inferior to superior sites (<< F.rev_cov_heldout_inf_to_sup_grad_amp_ratio >> and << F.rev_cov_heldout_sup_to_inf_grad_amp_ratio >> when fitted on the inferior or the superior half), and its magnetometer correlations and per-channel variances were poorly predicted. The magnetometers' excess over the model lay in a few far-field components (Section~S3).

For detectability, this excess favored Neuromag: with its measured covariance, Neuromag's detectability was << F.rev_cov_det_combined_corrected_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_det_combined_corrected_ci >> times its modeled value (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}A), a factor as large as the dense array's whole advantage. What this implies for the OPM depends on its real noise, which no measurement here constrains (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}B).

