For the dense array, the fall with depth was geometric. It was present with sensor noise alone (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}A, dotted) and in the peak field itself (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}B), and brain noise multiplied the ratio by a nearly constant factor in every bin (<< F.g2_brainmult_dense_vs_combined_range >>). On the cortex (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}C), the dense array was ahead almost everywhere, most of all on the lateral convexity.

By region (Fig.~\ref{fig:regions}), the dense advantage was largest in the frontal lobe (<< F.g2_lobe_frontal_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_lobe_frontal_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>) and smallest in the cingulate (<< F.g2_lobe_cingulate_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_lobe_cingulate_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>) and the insula (<< F.g2_lobe_insula_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>). Of the regions chosen from the epilepsy literature, the precentral gyrus favored the dense array most (<< F.reg_precentral_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>) and the parahippocampal cortex least (<< F.reg_parahippocampal_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>), although << F.reg_dk_dense_vs_combined_ib_below_parahippocampal_count >> atlas parcels, mostly cingulate and medial, had smaller advantages. The site-matched array trailed most on ventral and polar surfaces, such as the frontal pole (<< F.reg_dk_frontalpole_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>), the medial and lateral orbitofrontal cortex (<< F.reg_dk_medialorbitofrontal_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> and << F.reg_dk_lateralorbitofrontal_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>) and the temporal pole (<< F.reg_dk_temporalpole_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>), and, among the literature's regions, in the mesial temporal cortex (parahippocampal and entorhinal; << F.reg_mesial_temporal_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>).

These rankings are exploratory point estimates under the modeled noise. Under scenario D (Neuromag as measured, the OPM as modeled), the dense ratio became << F.wr_cov_s4_dense_lobe_cingulate_ratio >> in the cingulate, << F.wr_cov_s4_dense_lobe_parietal_ratio >> in the parietal, << F.wr_cov_s4_dense_lobe_frontal_ratio >> in the frontal and << F.wr_cov_s4_dense_lobe_temporal_ratio >> in the temporal lobe (point estimates). Under the same scenario, which includes the room field, the dense bin medians fell below 1 from the << F.wr_cov_s4_dense_depth_first_below1_label >>~mm bin.

