\subsection{Helmet fit versus head size}\label{sec:res-fit}

We answer this question with one primary quantity, the interaction: how much more the fixed helmet favors a smaller head than the adult, measured against the helmet fitted at the adult's gap (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}). Because it compares two Neuromag helmets around the same OPM array, it does not depend on the assumed OPM noise. The interaction was << F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_interaction_range >>~dB in the scaled adults and templates, every interval above zero (per head in Table~S6), and << F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_interaction_top_range >>~dB with the helmet fitted at the adult's top-contact gap. In each of these heads, the fitted helmet restored to Neuromag what the fixed helmet's gap and placement took away: the within-head contrast (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}D) was << F.rev_cgap_templates_scaled_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib_range >>~dB at the adult's gap and << F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_within_top_range >>~dB at its top-contact gap.

The interaction exceeds the heads' own within-head contrasts because the adult's contrast is negative, << F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib >>~dB: the fitted helmet holds the adult laterally centered, a looser pose than top contact. At the adult's top-contact gap, its contrast is << F.wr_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib >>~dB, the effect of the construction itself.

Of the excess advantage that the scaled adults and templates showed in the fixed helmet (<< F.g3b_templates_scaled_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB), little remained. Fitted at the adult's gap, their $\Delta$ against the adult fitted the same way was << F.rev_cgap_templates_scaled_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB (point estimates; interval bounds << F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_fitted_delta_ci_bounds_range >>~dB). Every template interval included zero, and only the school-age scaled adult's lay just above it; the highest value was that of the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template, which the MRI check classed as << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_verdict >>. Fitted at the adult's top-contact gap, these heads kept << F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_fittedtop_vs_top_range >>~dB against the adult at top contact (<< F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_fittedtop_vs_top_n_ci_above0 >> intervals above zero). With the room field projected out, every smaller head's point estimate at the adult's gap lay below the adult's (<< F.rev_cgap_smaller_fitted_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta_range >>~dB; << F.wr_cgap_fitted_proj_delta_n_ci_includes_zero_words >> of the eight intervals including zero).

In the children, provisional examples (Section~\ref{sec:heads}), $\Delta$ at the adult's gap was negative in A and B (<< F.rev_cgap_child_a_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.rev_cgap_child_a_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >> and << F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >>~dB). Fitted at the adult's top-contact gap and set against the adult at top contact, however, it was not established (<< F.rev_cgap_child_a_fittedtop_vs_fixed_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.rev_cgap_child_a_fittedtop_vs_fixed_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >> and << F.rev_cgap_child_b_fittedtop_vs_fixed_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.rev_cgap_child_b_fittedtop_vs_fixed_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >>~dB; Section~S5), and their within-head contrasts were << F.rev_cgap_child_a_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib >> and << F.rev_cgap_child_b_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib >>~dB. In child C, whose two gaps nearly coincide (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}A), $\Delta$ was << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >>~dB, and its within-head contrast, << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib >> << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib_ci >>~dB, matched the effect of the construction itself (<< F.wr_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib >> << F.wr_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib_ci >>~dB in the adult at an equal median gap).

None of these quantities apportions the effects exactly, because the fitted helmet changes the gap distribution, the placement and the sensors' coverage together. In the fitted helmet and at equal OPM site count, $\Delta$ was << F.rev_cgap_smaller_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB (Section~S5). In the scaled adults and templates, their fewer OPM sites offset a similar amount, whereas in children A and B no such gain was established (<< F.rev_cgap_child_a_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.rev_cgap_child_a_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >> and << F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >>~dB). In this model, fitting the helmet to the adult's median gap thus removed most of the excess advantage that the scaled adults and templates showed in the fixed adult helmet.

