\subsection{Limitations}\label{sec:limitations}

This study has limitations. First, no measurement validates the OPM covariance: the OPM sensor noise is declared and white, and Neuromag's measured noise alone favors Neuromag by as much as the dense array's whole advantage. A measured OPM noise covariance in the same band, under recording conditions matched to those of the recording that calibrates the model, would test the predicted advantage, and public OPM recordings could give a first check. Second, a single projection stands in for the preprocessing of real systems \citep{taulu2006,tierney2021}, which applying each system's own interference suppression would test. Third, one adult is the reference (its $D$ spans << F.g3b_adult_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >>~dB over the << F.g3b_n_family_placements >> source-blind placements), and the pediatric results rest on << F.wr_n_scaled_words >> copies of it, << F.wr_n_templates_words >> templates and << F.wr_n_children_words >> children from one dataset. The parcel intervals exclude between-subject variability, which a cohort of individual pediatric MRIs would measure. Fourth, the children's skull is modeled and their fiducials are transferred, and the MRI check cannot verify the white surface within a few millimeters of the scalp. Nor can it detect a head boundary that the T1 images themselves place too deep, which could explain both the shallow cortex and the dataset's own inner skull lying so close to the scalp. The children's negative $\Delta$ at the adult's gap may rest on that near-scalp cortex, which is why we treat them as provisional examples; individual pediatric MRIs with an independently measured scalp and skull and digitized fiducials would test it. Fifth, the templates' scalps lie << F.wr_qc_templates_offset_outside_otsu_mm_range >>~mm outside their MRI head boundaries and the adult's << F.wr_qc_adult_offset_inside_otsu_mm >>~mm inside, so sensors sit farther from a template's brain than from the adult's. The templates' $D$ and $\Delta$, including $\Delta$ in the fitted helmet, may therefore carry a difference of conventions of << F.wr_qc_template_adult_convention_mm_range >>~mm that could make them too low; for illustration only, a << F.g2_opm_gap_small_mm >>-mm OPM gap lowers the adult's dense ratio from << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> to << F.g2_sens_gap3mm_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>. The within-head contrast compares two helmets on one scalp, and the interaction is a difference of two such contrasts, so both are less exposed to it. The << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template's white surface also fits its T1 image best after a rigid shift of about << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_white_best_shift_mm >>~mm, a possibly displaced cortex or only the weak contrast of an average image. Such a shift brings part of the cortex closer to the sensors and part farther, so even the sign of its effect on this template's $\Delta$ is not obvious; it was not estimated. Template scalps derived from the templates' own MRI head boundaries, and a re-registered 18-month surface, would remove both questions. Sixth, the smaller heads keep the adult's background variance per unit cortical area. With that variance multiplied by << F.g3b_cfg_bg_factor_low >> or << F.g3b_cfg_bg_factor_high >>, every smaller head's median $D$ still exceeded the adult's in the fixed helmet (by << F.g3b_smaller_bg0p5_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_range >> and << F.g3b_smaller_bg2_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_range >>~dB), but measured pediatric brain noise would settle it. Seventh, all sources lie on the cortical surface, so the hippocampus and amygdala are absent and would need volume sources. Finally, the spike simulations are idealized: the detector's templates, candidate patterns and whitening covariance come from the simulation's own stationary model, and the null data hold no physiological transients. The ranking was tested against one declared mismatch under the primary noise model only, and head motion was bounded separately rather than simulated with the spikes (Section~S7). Recordings of real or phantom spikes with both systems would test the detection result.

